On macOS, file-posix's block status read a file kept compressed by the
file system (decmpfs) as one trailing hole: lseek(SEEK_DATA) answers
ENXIO at every offset of such a file, because its data fork is empty.
The App Store's installer leaves an app's files that way, so pflash's
blk_pread_nonzeroes() skipped the whole firmware image and the
Windows 11 on Arm machine's flash was all zeros (its CPU spun at the
exception vector, "Guest has not initialized the display (yet)").
A compressed file now reports no information, which reads it as data.
Drop when upstream handles UF_COMPRESSED in find_allocation().

diff --git a/block/file-posix.c b/block/file-posix.c
index 3c985da..9a37832 100644
--- a/block/file-posix.c
+++ b/block/file-posix.c
@@ -3185,6 +3185,19 @@ static int find_allocation(BlockDriverState *bs, off_t start,
      */
     offs = lseek(s->fd, start, SEEK_DATA);
     if (offs < 0) {
+#if defined(__APPLE__) && defined(UF_COMPRESSED)
+        /*
+         * macOS answers ENXIO for every offset of a file kept compressed
+         * (decmpfs, as the App Store's installer leaves a bundle's
+         * files): its data fork is empty. That is not a trailing hole;
+         * we learned nothing (D4).
+         */
+        struct stat st;
+        if (errno == ENXIO && fstat(s->fd, &st) == 0 &&
+            (st.st_flags & UF_COMPRESSED)) {
+            return -ENOTSUP;
+        }
+#endif
         return -errno;          /* D3 or D4 */
     }
 
